#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "7cec344e-596a-51ce-87cc-d0e44dc48df4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7118" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in\\left[0,1\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad\ny(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0b2d1f-df55-56cf-9ae9-ccef499e2352" :question/math-academy-id "e-9517" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x + z\\,\\mathrm{d}y + y\\,\\mathrm{d}z,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos t\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\sin t\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) =2t, \\qquad \ny(t) = \\cos t , \\qquad \nz(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}\\,dx+z\\,dy+y\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2t)^{3}\\cdot (2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}+({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "c18fbc4a-24d5-517d-9a3c-949a11787076" :question/math-academy-id "e-9528" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x -x\\,\\mathrm{d}y ,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{t|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5f4dd46-3506-5555-bb12-0be2c604bd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336981} {:answer/id #uuid "c170e8f6-f51b-5ef9-9b9c-28b91606e94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336982} {:answer/id #uuid "61aae4e1-cf91-530a-b7d0-f0a22450d212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336983} {:answer/id #uuid "364a4f31-9048-52dc-a24d-f28014de59b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336984} {:answer/id #uuid "88c28e57-b9fa-54be-af1f-4ee8884859ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c170e8f6-f51b-5ef9-9b9c-28b91606e94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336982} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9dca487-2c50-5d30-aa7d-d8e2864f5926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a58dfe4-c80f-5943-8159-e20f4642d770" :question/math-academy-id "q-124721" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(t-1)\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)\\cdot (1)+t\\cdot (1)+{e}^{t}\\cdot ({e}^{t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}+2t-1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+1-1)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1ac0f6-8da0-5e3a-a21c-0bddafd16848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336988} {:answer/id #uuid "8867512a-3a84-5f89-a1a4-d3b9f879ef8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336989} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad45ba-3c98-5a34-a103-02d62efded6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t+1\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336990} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b4bbb9-9a4c-53e2-8581-e46c7f6c8d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336991} {:answer/id #uuid "b09948ac-44ef-5e9d-a1c8-91614e52a6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b1ac0f6-8da0-5e3a-a21c-0bddafd16848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336988} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e586c51-8ccd-5953-b73b-4c0efdaea965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb7ae2bd-0784-56ff-b58a-eea210c6700b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124809" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}\\,dx+z\\,dy+y\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}\\,dx+z\\,dy+y\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\cdot (1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{t}^{3}+{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032e2a1e-eefd-51bb-84cd-9d2c54603206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{t}^{2}+3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336995} {:answer/id #uuid "cec41933-97a0-5e8f-ad55-0dca03248247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{t}^{3}-9\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336996} {:answer/id #uuid "3439b6c0-4c05-58a2-84b0-65880b6e61a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{t}^{4}-2\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336997} {:answer/id #uuid "b263f413-c6b1-5209-90fe-0dc5aed8fb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{t}^{2}+3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336998} {:answer/id #uuid "b21468b5-00c4-5489-9e2a-6555d43826f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cec41933-97a0-5e8f-ad55-0dca03248247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{t}^{3}-9\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336996} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8bbe138-306d-5251-ac89-a72690e8356a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5839d56-f6d8-5d06-95f8-dd9c646ba1f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124823" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}z\\,dx+y\\,dy-x\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2},\\,z(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z\\,dx+y\\,dy-x\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-x\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(z\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-x\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot (2t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{t}^{3}-9{sin}^{2}⁡t-9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{t}^{3}-9\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b69a3c39-9526-5be7-b7ea-fc888875d9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337002} {:answer/id #uuid "8bce23a0-140e-5961-b337-7497156db4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337003} {:answer/id #uuid "c040dc44-f8d0-5928-b803-dc570daf2d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}t+2\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337004} {:answer/id #uuid "fabafa75-9e7c-5ef7-9e34-b255191d8913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{t}^{2}+9\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337005} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1281db-a9f3-5673-b18e-5d639c9f8e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{t}^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c040dc44-f8d0-5928-b803-dc570daf2d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}t+2\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337004} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df17946-a5ca-5962-b4d9-4a316df98bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ffc1b85-360b-5d52-b60e-3d001d7bff89" :question/math-academy-id "q-124832" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}t+2\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fbda87b-ba93-5d6e-9d03-fe957f64db27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}{t}^{3}+2\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5e601a-faf4-50b6-a7ab-709ad9ee98ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}2{t}^{4}-5t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337010} {:answer/id #uuid "447aaf43-fb1d-5efc-ae9c-ecf39337b403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}{t}^{2}+10t+5\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337011} {:answer/id #uuid "2165de66-752f-5508-987b-b68c638d459f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}4{t}^{2}+11t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337012} {:answer/id #uuid "67c5a40f-e222-58c9-a0f4-ce24321fe456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}4t+5\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2165de66-752f-5508-987b-b68c638d459f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}4{t}^{2}+11t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337012} :answer-field/id #uuid "05811525-dcfe-5c17-bf64-c70e8c386c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38abb4d1-bac7-5e9e-bd43-53ae0b7ce435" :question/math-academy-id "q-124865" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=t\\,i+2t\\,j+3t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,5]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,z(t)=3t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j+3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}({y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}4{t}^{2}\\cdot (1)+t\\cdot (2)+3t\\cdot (3)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}4{t}^{2}+11t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd967b6-defd-518a-9713-edf26a3f6379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}3t^{2} + 2dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177671} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5abaf6-94fd-5705-85f2-f0ee69c7b76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}t^{3} - 2\\ln tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177673} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf71bf1-ca06-54ad-ab7e-527d975daca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}t^{2} + 5\\ln tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177674} {:answer/id #uuid "7c34fd97-c8e2-5fac-abd6-06191a98e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}5\\ln t + 2dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177675} {:answer/id #uuid "adf2428c-afe9-5781-be68-302512a63198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}6t^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adf2428c-afe9-5781-be68-302512a63198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}6t^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b5b1f8-2205-55dd-8481-133a50f9b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "416f9a6e-1aee-5dd5-ad70-acf59db83c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-124871" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along $C$, $x(t)=1$, $y(t)=t$, and $z(t)=t^2$, so $x'(t)=0$, $y'(t)=1$, and $z'(t)=2t$. Substitute both the coordinates and their derivatives:\n\n$$\\int_C 2y\\,dx+\\ln x\\,dy+3z\\,dz\n=\\int_1^3\\left(2t\\cdot0+\\ln(1)\\cdot1+3t^2\\cdot2t\\right)\\,dt\n=\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the equivalent integral is $\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt$, and its value is $[\\tfrac32t^4]_1^3=120$.\n\nThe original source accepted $\\int_1^3 6t^2\\,dt$ and its worked solution changed $3t^2\\cdot2t$ into $6t^2$. That simplification loses a factor of $t$. The study choice replaces that exponent with $3$; all other original choices are retained."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5188eab9-baa2-5efe-8117-464775f0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-3t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337016} {:answer/id #uuid "07a2efde-37a2-5563-8e47-b8782b606334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-4t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337017} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4436e0-994d-5a91-a100-17c228098560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-5t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337018} {:answer/id #uuid "14e91ed0-1420-5055-b30e-e6aea08bde47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}8{t}^{2}+4t+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337019} {:answer/id #uuid "83465644-4be0-5400-bdbb-f631b1da8427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-3t-8{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5188eab9-baa2-5efe-8117-464775f0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-3t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337016} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f373be-fc61-5ca1-83f8-65042ce99b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72ed363a-5d1e-5655-b35c-7247ea7c02ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-124872" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i-2{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t+1,\\,y(t)=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+(-4t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-2y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}((x-2y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(t+1-2\\cdot (-2{t}^{2}))\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot (-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t+1+4{t}^{2}-4{t}^{2}-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-3t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0f65502-6b25-5400-8d15-d626498c0a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{t}^{3}-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} {:answer/id #uuid "132876a1-4518-512e-8ce9-ebcdc2ce95c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{t}^{3}-t-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337024} {:answer/id #uuid "db973b1b-cb55-54d1-899f-25067da84d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{t}^{3}+t-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337025} {:answer/id #uuid "4738f29a-065f-5348-b749-bfeb1c6e07aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}t(4{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337026} {:answer/id #uuid "49584ac4-ce58-5bf7-8564-0dc7120c97f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{t}^{3}-3t-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132876a1-4518-512e-8ce9-ebcdc2ce95c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{t}^{3}-t-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337024} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c677cc0-8fb7-5cfc-8d08-ba9d17bdabf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87d7259c-ca26-53c7-b47d-c8aa49f4ead9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124875" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x+2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=(t-2)\\,i+({t}^{3}-t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-2,\\,y(t)={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x+2)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x+2)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+2)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(t-2+{t}^{3}-t)\\cdot 1+(t-2+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-2+3{t}^{3}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{t}^{3}-t-2\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "597eca5e-f978-5002-9f37-22d95d74e8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337030} {:answer/id #uuid "d52ebe83-b16f-5952-b1f7-8c9475ec83d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337031} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cec9ba-e608-5357-984d-3798d5e791f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337032} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe347ea-7baa-52d6-bb60-57e9c7ce4329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337033} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e0f538-4142-5ba6-8ff1-5861135387d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d52ebe83-b16f-5952-b1f7-8c9475ec83d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337031} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e12cdab-144a-5862-9123-a2c0e7effe26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6c4d7e0-6d3a-5784-88f9-be750756f8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124878" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(y-x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(y-x)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(x-y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(y-x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}((x-y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(y-x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8012cb3-861d-5c6c-b36b-316b61f631cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337037} {:answer/id #uuid "7c46a4c4-4404-5cdd-b483-54474a7748ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t+1}+{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337038} {:answer/id #uuid "10a688bd-9f86-5b4f-8d7f-28137a29326e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337039} {:answer/id #uuid "4e634465-c33b-5166-8048-28452b316f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337040} {:answer/id #uuid "e1253b91-011b-5d6d-9242-cd2b3741bafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c46a4c4-4404-5cdd-b483-54474a7748ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t+1}+{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337038} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3cd091-bba1-5cdc-9ad3-b8d51c88b7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1c03e17-fa10-5a92-95ff-3965b57742a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124882" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{t+1}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t+1})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t+1}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t+1}+{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f905835-4673-54fc-9a76-954a46c59501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337044} {:answer/id #uuid "708b577c-1b87-58d4-89c2-5fb7858bd4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337045} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1cba29-5903-508b-80d1-efe1081431fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337046} {:answer/id #uuid "511d32a8-5f44-557f-8d54-9cc9da66582c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337047} {:answer/id #uuid "36f39c70-7626-5242-ae8b-a57198b21e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "511d32a8-5f44-557f-8d54-9cc9da66582c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337047} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aed3c9b-fead-59af-86bb-5518a1d6a05d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20c4e7d1-de84-56b1-8cd9-a50360665597" :question/math-academy-id "q-124885" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{t},\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(x{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot {t}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{t})}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{t}+t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d28e9ad5-9558-5dda-96be-31998cf5dce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337051} {:answer/id #uuid "58b9bd7d-b0ea-5cdb-8098-f6d118d13baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337052} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3c1817-cf56-5d30-8501-d679b1be4793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337053} {:answer/id #uuid "c623b031-0d1d-56f4-96ff-5e81ca76ba21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337054} {:answer/id #uuid "534bb052-2e3e-5099-9546-a63a7d380704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58b9bd7d-b0ea-5cdb-8098-f6d118d13baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337052} :answer-field/id #uuid "7025f285-c5f5-556e-91a7-61e33f34bd08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7af25f1a-9124-52a0-83a0-6d803e2bac66" :question/math-academy-id "q-81097" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the circle parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be78d9bc-0353-5eab-995a-b71c8c780db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337058} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cd8746-a7b8-5bec-9351-f8031bd90ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337059} {:answer/id #uuid "0832d413-1eb6-54ad-846c-5acaacccdf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337060} {:answer/id #uuid "b374a0cc-1570-51ec-8c6e-f5cb43e1ab75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337061} {:answer/id #uuid "09d03aa1-4d25-57b2-bced-15fd4f450c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be78d9bc-0353-5eab-995a-b71c8c780db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337058} :answer-field/id #uuid "8937c22f-332c-5acc-ba22-d73f4d9b3af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9869daf-0562-5202-8bf9-2c81e380f815" :question/math-academy-id "q-81171" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{(2x+y+1)}{x}\\,dx+dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2t+{t}^{2}-1+1}{t}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+t+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[2t+\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2(2)+\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[4+6] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece0917c-3d63-5e94-9bd6-39f84810fe95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337065} {:answer/id #uuid "446b2787-bb2d-55cf-a7af-c06113b29207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337066} {:answer/id #uuid "e8baa50f-ad43-5e7c-be1d-104e6c1d9d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337067} {:answer/id #uuid "34d7d6dc-6baf-5361-a33d-7d59c7a46b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337068} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7f9683-6e99-55eb-adb7-c1c410779747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "446b2787-bb2d-55cf-a7af-c06113b29207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337066} :answer-field/id #uuid "c51450c4-28c4-5436-91f0-a6432b3850b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152c261f-360a-52bd-b3d4-387741e2a06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81177" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){|}_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3f1d29e-e4f4-5e47-994d-e772d5e70597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337072} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2a3b17-8aa1-5f6f-acb3-b3120a8ded72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337073} {:answer/id #uuid "8f92d9ca-8eb8-5421-8772-ea7fe7d6651a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337074} {:answer/id #uuid "692743d4-e03d-5ece-9f07-7a77fa18947f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337075} {:answer/id #uuid "ddacda9b-0383-5939-8d3d-b9877cd71303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f92d9ca-8eb8-5421-8772-ea7fe7d6651a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337074} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd9ea258-9454-55c0-a355-a1fc0a48e1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fec06d68-180e-5575-8694-e1107dfa1adf" :question/math-academy-id "q-90844" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,dx+x\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,z(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,dx+x\\,dy+dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{z}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{z}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
